<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manfredo Fanti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manfredo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fanti</a> was an Italian general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>, born in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>. Exiled after the revolt of 1831, he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s Carlist War and then served the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. In 1859 he commanded the army of the United Provinces of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and in 1860 he led the Sardinian invasion of the Papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marches</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> before becoming minister of war and organising the new Italian army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manfredo-fanti.jpg" alt="Manfredo Fanti" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manf.Fanti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manfredo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fanti</a> was born at Carpi on 23 February 1806 and educated at the military college of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>. Implicated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciro-menotti">Ciro Menotti</a>'s revolutionary movement of 1831, he was condemned to death and hanged in effigy, but escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and served in the French engineers. In 1833 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>'s failed attempt to invade Savoy, and from 1835 he spent thirteen years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in Queen Christina's army against the Carlists, rising to a high staff post.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When war broke out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1848 he returned and was given a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> brigade, and his courage and tact steadied the situation in the confusion after Charles Albert's retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Elected to the Piedmontese Chamber of Deputies in 1849, he again commanded a Lombard brigade under General Girolamo Ramorino. Distrusted as a Mazzinian and soldier of fortune, he was court-martialled for those operations and acquitted, but not employed again until the Crimean expedition of 1855.</p><p>In 1859 he commanded the 2nd Division at Palestro, Magenta and Solferino. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villafranca">Armistice of Villafranca</a> he turned the army of the United Provinces of Central Italy into a drilled force of 45,000 men, steering between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bettino-ricasoli">Bettino Ricasoli</a>'s caution and the impetuosity of his second-in-command <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, who resigned. Leading two army corps into the Papal States, he took Ancona and defeated the Papal army at Castelfidardo, capturing General Lamoricière; in three weeks he conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and took 28,000 prisoners. In the Neapolitan campaign he served as chief of staff under the king, defeated a Neapolitan force at Mola and organised the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As minister of war and marine under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fanti</a> merged the league's army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>'s and reformed the army, clashing with Alfonso La Marmora. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s wish to keep his volunteer army: the Esercito Meridionale was disbanded and only 1,700 of its 7,000 officers entered the new, conscription-based Royal Italian Army, for which the Left criticised him severely. He resigned on Cavour's death on 7 June 1861, and many of his reforms were reversed by later ministers. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 5 April 1865 and is known as the founder of the Royal Italian Army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manfredo Fanti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their provisional governments formed a league with a common army under General <b>Manfredo Fanti</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-provinces-of-central-italy">United Provinces of Central Italy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1844" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Manfredo Fanti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
